    id: "gen-2026-10-22-04",
    slug: "bob-password-protected-pdf",
    title: "BOB Password Protected Statement PDF to Excel Guide",
    excerpt: "Bank of Baroda locks email and app statements behind a password. Learn the common BOB password formats, open the file safely, and convert it to Excel.",
    featureImage: "/blog/bob-password-protected-pdf.webp",
    author: "David Chen, CPA",
    tags: ["bob", "bank of baroda", "password protected pdf", "tax"],
    published: true,
    createdAt: "2026-10-22T06:30:00.000Z",
    updatedAt: "2026-10-22T06:30:00.000Z",
    content: "<p>If your Bank of Baroda statements arrive by email or through the BOB World app, you already know the monthly routine: the PDF is locked and asks for a password before you can read a single transaction. Learning to convert a <strong>BOB password protected statement PDF to Excel</strong> is the fastest way to move past that lock screen and turn a static document into data you can actually use — sorted transactions for expense tracking, clean totals for tax filing, and no more retyping line after line by hand. In this guide, we cover where BOB sends its password-protected statements, the password patterns the bank commonly uses, how to open the file safely, and how to convert it into a spreadsheet in minutes with a file-based converter.</p>\n<h2>Where BOB sends password-protected statements</h2>\n<p>Bank of Baroda issues password-protected statements in three main places. The most common is the monthly e-statement: if you registered an email address with the bank, a statement PDF arrives automatically each month, and that attachment is locked. The second is the BOB World mobile app — under the account or statement section you can download a statement for any date range, and the downloaded file carries the same password protection. The third is BOB internet banking, where statements pulled from the account summary behave the same way.</p>\n<p>The lock is there for a good reason. A statement shows your account number, balances, and every transaction, so an unlocked PDF sitting in an inbox would be a gift to anyone who gets into your email. Password protection means the file is useless to anyone who does not know the format — but it also means you need to know the format yourself before you can do anything with the data. Our <a href=\"/banks/bank-of-baroda\">Bank of Baroda converter page</a> has more background on working with BOB files.</p>\n<h2>How to open a password-protected BOB statement safely</h2>\n<p>Before converting anything, you need the file open — converters read tables, not locked files, so the password step comes first. Bank of Baroda has historically based its statement passwords on details you already gave the bank. Commonly seen patterns include the first four letters of your registered name (sometimes in uppercase) followed by your date of birth in DDMMYYYY format, or digits from your registered mobile number. Treat these as common patterns, not guarantees: the exact format can vary by statement type and by when the statement was generated. Sometimes the email that delivered the statement hints at the format; if nothing works, the bank's official help channels or your branch can confirm the current one.</p>\n<p>Open the PDF only on a device you trust, and resist the temptation to keep unlocked copies in your inbox \"for later.\" A safer habit: unlock the statement, convert it, and then delete the unlocked copy. For a look at how another large bank handles this, see our guide to <a href=\"/blog/icici-bank-password-protected-pdf\">ICICI's password-protected statements</a>.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"/blog/bob-password-protected-pdf-inline-1.webp\" alt=\"BOB password protected statement PDF to Excel — downloading a statement on a phone\" width=\"1200\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" /><figcaption>Downloading your statement straight from the phone</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>Converting your BOB statement PDF to Excel</h2>\n<p>Once the PDF opens with the password, conversion is straightforward. Upload the unlocked file to a file-based statement converter — <a href=\"/\">Convert Statement</a> reads the PDF's underlying table structure and rebuilds it as a real spreadsheet: dates land in a true date column, amounts stay numeric so SUM and SUMIF formulas work, and every transaction row is preserved in order. Your first 8 pages convert free with no credit card required, which covers most monthly statements.</p>\n<p>The process takes about a minute: select the unlocked BOB PDF, let the converter extract the tables, and download the Excel file. Because the file is now data instead of a document, you can sort, filter, and search it — things a locked PDF simply cannot do. If you also hold accounts elsewhere, the same workflow applies; our <a href=\"/blog/axis-bank-password-protected-pdf\">Axis Bank password-protected PDF guide</a> walks through an identical process for Axis statements.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"/blog/bob-password-protected-pdf-inline-2.webp\" alt=\"BOB password protected statement PDF to Excel — organizing monthly expenses in a spreadsheet for taxes\" width=\"1200\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" /><figcaption>From locked PDF to a working spreadsheet</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>Turning the spreadsheet into expense tracking and tax-ready records</h2>\n<p>This is where the Excel file earns its keep. Add a category column — groceries, rent, utilities, travel, subscriptions — and you have a month-by-month picture of spending that no PDF can give you. Filter by payee to spot forgotten subscriptions, use a pivot table to total each category per month, and compare months side by side to see trends before they become problems.</p>\n<p>At tax time, the same sheet becomes your source of truth. Filter the category column for deductible expenses, total them with a single formula, and hand your preparer clean numbers instead of a stack of PDFs. One habit makes all of this far more useful: convert every month's statement the day it arrives. Twelve small conversions a year beat one painful cleanup every April, and the running balance column in each export lets you verify each file against its PDF in seconds.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<p><strong>What is the password for my Bank of Baroda statement PDF?</strong><br />BOB commonly bases statement passwords on your registered name and date of birth — for example, the first four letters of your name plus your birth date — but the exact format varies by statement type and generation date, so treat these as common patterns rather than a guaranteed recipe. The delivery email sometimes hints at the format, and the bank's official channels can confirm it.</p>\n<p><strong>Can I convert the PDF without unlocking it first?</strong><br />No. A file-based converter needs to read the statement's tables, and it cannot do that while the file is locked. Unlock the PDF with the correct password on your own device first, then upload the unlocked file for conversion.</p>\n<p><strong>Is it safe to upload my bank statement for conversion?</strong><br />Convert on trusted services that process files in memory and delete them immediately after conversion rather than storing them. As a rule, unlock the file, convert it, download the Excel, and delete both the unlocked PDF and the original email attachment afterward.</p>\n<p><strong>Will the Excel file contain all my transactions?</strong><br />Yes — a proper conversion preserves every transaction row with its date, description, and amount, plus the running balance column. Compare the opening and closing balances in the spreadsheet against the PDF; if they match, the conversion is complete and accurate.</p>\n<p>Ready to put your locked BOB statements to work? Upload your first unlocked statement on the <a href=\"/\">Convert Statement homepage</a> and download it as Excel in about a minute — and if you convert statements regularly, check the <a href=\"/pricing\">pricing page</a> for plans that cover higher volumes.</p>",
